"Germs are tiny living things, such as bacteria and viruses, that are too small to see. Most bacteria are harmless, and many, like the ones in your gut, even help you. But some can make you sick. Your body is attacked by germs every day, and it wins almost every time, usually without you noticing. It does this with three walls of defense. The first wall keeps germs out. Your skin is a tough, dry barrier that most germs cannot get through. Your nose, mouth, and throat are lined with sticky mucus that traps germs, and tiny hairs sweep the mucus away so you can swallow or sneeze it out. Tears and saliva contain substances that kill bacteria, and your stomach is filled with strong acid. Germs get in mainly through cuts, or through your eyes, nose, and mouth, often from your own hands. If germs get past the first wall, the second wall attacks right away. Bacteria can double every 20 minutes, so speed matters. Damaged cells send out alarm signals, and blood vessels nearby widen and leak fluid. That is why a cut turns red, warm, swollen, and sore. Then white blood cells squeeze out of the blood vessels and hunt the germs, swallowing them whole. A single drop of blood carries hundreds of thousands of white blood cells. A fever can help too, because many germs grow more slowly when your body is warmer. The third wall is the smartest. Special white blood cells study the invader and make antibodies, Y-shaped proteins that fit only that one germ, like a key fits a lock. Antibodies stick to the germs, making them easier to catch and destroy. The first time your body meets a new germ, making the right antibodies can take a week or more, so you may feel sick for days. But your body remembers. After the fight, memory cells stay on guard for years. If the same germ comes back, they make antibodies in a day or two, and the germ is often gone before you feel sick at all. Vaccines use this memory. A vaccine shows your body a harmless piece or a weakened form of a germ, so your body learns to recognize it without having to get sick first. You can help your three walls. Wash your hands with soap for at least 20 seconds, especially before eating and after using the toilet. Cover coughs and sneezes with your elbow. Clean cuts and keep them covered. Sleep well and eat well, because a tired body fights germs less well. And remember that antibiotics kill bacteria but do nothing against viruses like colds and flu. Now try the germ calculator and the handwashing timer below the model.": "92bcb148a1f6",
